From an individual-autonomy perspective, moral life begins with the person and her freely chosen commitments rather than with externally imposed roles. The young woman’s choice is therefore intelligible only as an exercise of her own agency and priorities; any “obligation” to her mother has moral force solely because she herself endorses it. Consequently, framing the act as the discharge of a pre-existing family duty (A) carries little weight, while both the legitimacy of her personal growth (B) and the recognition that the decision is fundamentally non-moral in the traditional sense (C) carry substantial weight.  
For the second question the same logic applies: external hierarchies of duty or social judgment are secondary; what matters is the agent’s own anticipated regret or integrity.

